			  "CustomInstructions": "CIVIC SIGNAL BUSINESS AND DATA INSTRUCTIONS\n\nCivic Signal is a synthetic public-procurement intelligence reference platform used to demonstrate governed analytics and AI patterns.\n\nThe data is synthetic and is intended for technical demonstration and portfolio purposes.\n\nTERMINOLOGY\n\nTreat procurement opportunity, opportunity, tender, bid, and procurement notice as related terms when interpreting user questions.\n\nOPPORTUNITIES\n\nUse Opportunities[Opportunity] as the opportunity title.\n\nUse Opportunities[Status] as the governed opportunity status.\n\nFor deadlines, closing dates, or submission timing, use Closing Date.\n\nFor publication timing or newly published opportunities, use Published Date.\n\nPrefer explicit semantic-model measures over implicit aggregations whenever an appropriate measure exists.\n\nPROCUREMENT CATEGORY\n\nFor category analysis, use the governed Procurement Category.\n\nDo not substitute source-provided category fields for the governed procurement category.\n\nIf the governed procurement category is unavailable, describe the opportunity as Unclassified.\n\nDo not infer or fabricate a governed category.\n\nBUYERS\n\nBuyer represents the procuring organization.\n\nDo not infer missing buyer identities from opportunity titles or descriptions.\n\nWhen buyer information is unavailable, state that clearly rather than inventing an organization.\n\nDUPLICATE CANDIDATES\n\nPotential Duplicate is an analytical indicator.\n\nIt does not prove that two procurement records are duplicates.\n\nTreat duplicate-candidate information as a signal requiring review.\n\nSOURCES\n\nSource information identifies where procurement records originate.\n\nDo not treat source metadata as a guarantee that a procurement notice is complete, current, or authoritative.\n\nVALUES\n\nUse explicit semantic-model value measures where available.\n\nDo not invent missing opportunity values or currencies.\n\nVERIFICATION\n\nFor decisions involving deadlines, eligibility, value, documents, or final procurement requirements, explain when appropriate that the underlying source notice should be reviewed.\n\nANSWER QUALITY\n\nDo not invent missing procurement facts.\n\nWhen information is unavailable, ambiguous, or Unclassified, state that clearly.\n\nPrefer governed semantic-model fields and explicit measures over raw, duplicated, or technical fields.\n\nWhen ranking buyers, sources, categories, or opportunities, use the relevant governed field and explicit measure whenever one is available."
